.TH UBERTOOTH\-AFH 1 "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
.PP
.BR ubertooth-afh (1) 
\- passive detection of AFH channel map
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.RS
.nf
ubertooth\-afh \-u <uap> \-l <lap> [\-r] [\-m <count>]
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Classic Bluetooth piconets commonly make use of Adaptive Frequency
Hopping (AFH) to avoid interference with other signals in 2.4 GHz. This
tool attempts to determine the AFH map being used for a given piconet
identified by UAP and LAP.
.PP
At a minimum you must provide UAP and LAP. See 
.BR ubertooth-rx (1) 
for a
method for sniffing these over the air and details on where else to find
them. By default the tool will only print the AFH map when it is
updated. By providing the \fB\fC\-r\fR flag, the AFH map will be printed out in
binary form once per second.
.PP
Note: the \fB\fC\-a\fR option (initial AFH map) is currently non\-functional; its
handler is disabled in the source and the flag has no effect.
.SH OPTIONS
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-l <LAP>\fR :
LAP of target piconet (3 bytes / 6 hex digits)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-u <UAP>\fR :
UAP of target piconet (1 byte / 2 hex digits)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-m <int>\fR :
threshold for channel removal: a channel is marked unused once this many packets
arrive without it being seen; 1 or more (default: 5)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-r\fR :
print AFH channel map once every second (default: print on update)
.RE
.PP
Other options:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-t <seconds>\fR :
timeout for initial AFH map detection (not required); see DETECTION AND
MONITORING\&. No effect with \fB\fC\-r\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-e <0\-5>\fR :
maximum access code errors (default: 2, range: 0\-5)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-V\fR :
print version information
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-U <index|serial>\fR :
select an Ubertooth by enumeration index or serial number; see
.BR ubertooth (7),
SELECTING A DEVICE
.RE
.SH DETECTION AND MONITORING
.PP
\fB\fCubertooth\-afh\fR does not stop on its own. It keeps monitoring the
piconet and reporting changes to the map until it is interrupted (Ctrl\-C),
both with and without \fB\fC\-t\fR\&.
.PP
While it listens, the board hops across the channels the piconet has not been
seen on, and the tool tells the board to stop visiting a channel once a packet
from the piconet has arrived there. That is how the map fills in: a channel is
reported used only when a packet is received on it.
.PP
\fB\fC\-t <seconds>\fR only adds an initial detection phase: the tool listens
for that many seconds, prints the map it found, and then starts monitoring. If
that phase received no packet from the piconet there is no map to print, so the
tool says so, does not start monitoring, and exits with status 2 instead of
printing an all\-zero map
(which reads as a detected map whose piconet uses no channel at all):
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ubertooth\-afh \-l 446688 \-u 22 \-t 3
error: no packets from ??:??:22:44:66:88 within 3 s; AFH map unknown, not monitoring
.fi
.RE
.PP
Nothing is wrong with the board in that case: the piconet is out of range, it
is not transmitting, or the UAP/LAP is not the one in use. A longer
\fB\fC\-t\fR, or no \fB\fC\-t\fR at all, keeps listening.
.PP
To wait for a piconet that is not in range yet (start the tool, then bring the
devices close or start the connection), run without \fB\fC\-t\fR: monitoring
starts at once, waits as long as it takes, and prints
\fB\fC+ channel N is used now\fR as the piconet's packets arrive.
\fB\fC\-t\fR is for a bounded check that gives up when nothing is heard in time.
.PP
\fB\fC\-r\fR has its own loop, which prints the map once a second from the
start and ignores \fB\fC\-t\fR\&.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.PP
\fB\fCubertooth\-afh\fR takes options only. A word that is not an option, including
a bare \fB\fC\-\fR, is refused before any device is opened and named:
\fB\fCerror: unexpected argument '<word>'\fR, with exit status 1.
.PP
\fB\fC\-h\fR and \fB\fC\-V\fR end the command only once the whole command line has
parsed, so a stray word or a bad option value is reported even
alongside them.
.PP
An option value must be a whole value in its range, or it is refused the same
way instead of being read as 0, for example
\fB\fCerror: timeout must be a whole number of seconds, 0 for none (got abc)\fR\&.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.PP
\fB\fCubertooth\-afh\fR does not stop on its own, so it normally exits 0 after
being interrupted (Ctrl\-C).
.PP
It exits 2 when the \fB\fC\-t\fR detection phase received no packet from the
piconet (see DETECTION AND MONITORING). The number of seconds in that message
is the \fB\fC\-t\fR value, not the time actually spent listening, which is
shorter if the tool was interrupted during the phase.
.PP
It exits 1 without opening a device for a bad command line (a missing UAP or
LAP, an option value out of range, or a word that is not an option), and when
the selected Ubertooth is not found. Without \fB\fC\-r\fR, it also exits 1
when the receive could not be started at all; \fB\fC\-r\fR does not report
that and still exits 0.
